WEBVTT

00:00.180 --> 00:01.290
Instructor: So now that we've talked about

00:01.290 --> 00:04.830
remote tracking branch references, quite a mouthful there.

00:04.830 --> 00:07.830
Let's talk about working with remote branches.

00:07.830 --> 00:10.920
So for example, if we have a repo that we clone

00:10.920 --> 00:13.650
or that we wanna work with that has multiple branches

00:13.650 --> 00:17.700
on GitHub, how do we work with those branches locally?

00:17.700 --> 00:20.550
Let me demonstrate what I'm referring to.

00:20.550 --> 00:23.040
I'm gonna go to this repository I've made

00:23.040 --> 00:24.930
called Remote Branches Demo.

00:24.930 --> 00:27.540
You can find the link along with this video.

00:27.540 --> 00:29.340
I recommend you try this.

00:29.340 --> 00:31.890
So on the main branch, the default branch,

00:31.890 --> 00:32.970
we have two files.

00:32.970 --> 00:35.730
Cactus, with a nice cactus, ASCII art

00:35.730 --> 00:39.030
and Rose with Rose ASCII art.

00:39.030 --> 00:41.760
But there are four total branches.

00:41.760 --> 00:45.570
And when I clone this repository, I'll copy that URL.

00:45.570 --> 00:48.930
I'll go to my local machine, we'll zoom, zoom,

00:48.930 --> 00:50.430
we'll back out, sure.

00:50.430 --> 00:53.730
And to here, not a repo, git clone.

00:53.730 --> 00:55.503
And then this URL.

00:56.490 --> 00:59.880
Alright, so we now have this remote branches demo

00:59.880 --> 01:03.750
and if I type git status, I'm on the main branch.

01:03.750 --> 01:07.020
That's the default branch as we've seen.

01:07.020 --> 01:09.270
If instead the default was master,

01:09.270 --> 01:10.890
that's the branch I end up with.

01:10.890 --> 01:13.500
Whatever the default branch is when I clone,

01:13.500 --> 01:15.060
that's what I get.

01:15.060 --> 01:16.800
So what about these other branches?

01:16.800 --> 01:18.090
Do I have them here?

01:18.090 --> 01:19.470
Git branch.

01:19.470 --> 01:22.980
No, I only have the main branch.

01:22.980 --> 01:26.432
So how do I work on, for example,

01:26.432 --> 01:31.173
the food branch where we have banana and popsicle?

01:32.400 --> 01:36.210
Or how do I work on the movies branch

01:36.210 --> 01:39.480
where we have "Ghostbusters" and "Bambi"?

01:39.480 --> 01:41.670
Well, it's actually not bad at all.

01:41.670 --> 01:43.136
I'll demonstrate it here.

01:43.136 --> 01:45.360
So in our example on these slides,

01:45.360 --> 01:47.850
we have a master branch that's the default

01:47.850 --> 01:49.500
and a puppies branch.

01:49.500 --> 01:51.420
Both of these are on GitHub.

01:51.420 --> 01:53.543
So when I clone that project,

01:53.543 --> 01:57.390
I get all the history, all the data, but that does not mean

01:57.390 --> 01:59.640
that it's all in my workspace at once.

01:59.640 --> 02:02.340
So as we saw, when I typed git branch,

02:02.340 --> 02:05.490
we only saw main or in this example, master

02:05.490 --> 02:07.526
I don't see all these other branches.

02:07.526 --> 02:09.210
Just main.

02:09.210 --> 02:11.040
So what exactly is going on?

02:11.040 --> 02:14.313
Well if we look at the remote branches, remember the -r,

02:15.180 --> 02:16.380
it's a very different outcome.

02:16.380 --> 02:21.000
git branch -r, and we see every one of those branches.

02:21.000 --> 02:25.380
origin/fantasy, origin/food, main and movies.

02:25.380 --> 02:29.700
So my local repo, it knows about all of those branches

02:29.700 --> 02:32.220
and it has a remote tracking reference

02:32.220 --> 02:34.860
where it can tell me where each one of those is.

02:34.860 --> 02:36.390
Remember, they're just like a bookmark

02:36.390 --> 02:38.610
that says this is where fantasy is this is food,

02:38.610 --> 02:39.660
this is main.

02:39.660 --> 02:42.930
So we know about them here, my repo knows them

02:42.930 --> 02:46.710
but I don't see full fledged branches for them.

02:46.710 --> 02:48.780
So how do I make that happen?

02:48.780 --> 02:51.900
Well, we need to talk a little bit about the relationship

02:51.900 --> 02:55.980
between a full-fledged branch and a remote tracking branch.

02:55.980 --> 02:58.980
By default, when we clone a repository,

02:58.980 --> 03:00.510
let's say in this diagram,

03:00.510 --> 03:02.640
it's a master branch that we end up with.

03:02.640 --> 03:07.290
That master branch is automatically tracking, it's connected

03:07.290 --> 03:09.990
to the remote branch origin/master.

03:09.990 --> 03:12.570
They're both master, they have the same name.

03:12.570 --> 03:14.820
So that's the default behavior.

03:14.820 --> 03:16.260
I didn't make that happen.

03:16.260 --> 03:17.910
Every time we've cloned something,

03:17.910 --> 03:21.060
in our case it's main, on the main branch

03:21.060 --> 03:22.713
that we're currently on, whoops.

03:24.180 --> 03:25.807
We see this sort of message.

03:25.807 --> 03:28.170
"Up to date with origin/main."

03:28.170 --> 03:30.570
I did not make that connection myself.

03:30.570 --> 03:34.110
I didn't say, "Okay, Git, there's a main branch here

03:34.110 --> 03:37.740
and I want it to be connected to origin/main."

03:37.740 --> 03:39.210
That just happened.

03:39.210 --> 03:41.790
So we have these other branches that are remote.

03:41.790 --> 03:43.500
origin slash, in our case,

03:43.500 --> 03:48.480
it's what movies origin/food, origin/fantasy.

03:48.480 --> 03:51.090
In this example, origin/puppies.

03:51.090 --> 03:55.290
So how do I make a branch here that is connected

03:55.290 --> 03:57.150
to origin/puppies?

03:57.150 --> 03:58.662
I wanna work on that.

03:58.662 --> 04:01.470
So I could check out one of those branches.

04:01.470 --> 04:03.030
Check out origin/puppies

04:03.030 --> 04:06.120
or check out origin/ whatever we have.

04:06.120 --> 04:07.270
Let's just take a look.

04:08.640 --> 04:10.890
Let's do origin/food.

04:10.890 --> 04:11.910
So I can do that.

04:11.910 --> 04:13.653
Check out origin/food.

04:14.640 --> 04:16.260
Well I'm a liar, we can't do that.

04:16.260 --> 04:18.840
We need git checkout origin/food

04:18.840 --> 04:20.910
and now we're in detached head.

04:20.910 --> 04:24.690
But you can see banana.txt and popsicle

04:24.690 --> 04:26.400
and there they are in their glory.

04:26.400 --> 04:28.590
We've got banana and popsicle

04:28.590 --> 04:30.630
so I'm able to check those out,

04:30.630 --> 04:32.490
but I'm in detached head.

04:32.490 --> 04:33.570
It's not the same thing.

04:33.570 --> 04:35.040
It's what we talked about here,

04:35.040 --> 04:38.400
what I want is a branch that I can work on

04:38.400 --> 04:40.690
that is connected to origin/puppies.

04:40.690 --> 04:43.440
Rather than just checking out the remote branch,

04:43.440 --> 04:45.631
I want to actually have my own branch here

04:45.631 --> 04:47.850
that is connected to it.

04:47.850 --> 04:50.550
So the way we do this is actually super easy.

04:50.550 --> 04:52.680
Thanks to the new git switch command.

04:52.680 --> 04:56.970
We just switch to a branch with that exact same name.

04:56.970 --> 05:00.450
So if Git detects that we make a branch called puppies

05:00.450 --> 05:03.401
and there is already a remote branch called puppies,

05:03.401 --> 05:07.260
it's going to just assume that we want them connected.

05:07.260 --> 05:09.900
So we can change that and we could have a branch

05:09.900 --> 05:13.320
called puppies that actually is tracking a branch

05:13.320 --> 05:15.840
called origin/cats.

05:15.840 --> 05:18.960
We can do that ourself, but by default,

05:18.960 --> 05:21.120
we usually want them to be the same.

05:21.120 --> 05:23.790
When I do git switch puppies, git will make me

05:23.790 --> 05:26.483
a local puppies branch and it will automatically set it up

05:26.483 --> 05:30.600
to track the remote branch origin/puppies.

05:30.600 --> 05:34.470
So I end up with my own branch puppies and it's pointing

05:34.470 --> 05:37.440
or it's connected to origin/puppies in the same way

05:37.440 --> 05:40.020
that master is connected to origin/master

05:40.020 --> 05:43.110
or in the same way that main is connected to origin/main.

05:43.110 --> 05:46.830
So if I go back to main, as we've seen before,

05:46.830 --> 05:48.288
automatically connected.

05:48.288 --> 05:51.453
If I now switch to one of these branches,

05:52.290 --> 05:56.640
one of these remote branches that I don't have like movies,

05:56.640 --> 05:58.530
if I switch to movies, which again,

05:58.530 --> 06:00.510
we don't have that branch

06:00.510 --> 06:02.940
and normally, if I try and switch to a branch

06:02.940 --> 06:06.270
that doesn't exist here, we get this.

06:06.270 --> 06:07.710
Invalid reference.

06:07.710 --> 06:10.440
I can't just do git switch plants.

06:10.440 --> 06:12.060
There is no branch called plants.

06:12.060 --> 06:15.810
But if I do git switch movies, take a look at that.

06:15.810 --> 06:17.730
I now have a branch called movies,

06:17.730 --> 06:21.510
set up to track remote branch movies from origin

06:21.510 --> 06:23.310
and it switched me over.

06:23.310 --> 06:27.240
So now I'm on the movies branch and if I type git branch,

06:27.240 --> 06:29.463
we actually have movies in main.

06:30.300 --> 06:34.020
And if I take a look here, we've got "Bambi."

06:34.020 --> 06:35.760
Hello, Bambi. So cute.

06:35.760 --> 06:37.077
And "Ghostbusters."

06:38.070 --> 06:41.887
Type git status and you'll see,

06:41.887 --> 06:45.282
"Your branch is up to date with origin/movies."

06:45.282 --> 06:46.740
So we're now connected.

06:46.740 --> 06:48.990
This branch movies is referencing

06:48.990 --> 06:51.720
its tracking origin/movies.

06:51.720 --> 06:53.490
So the old way of doing this

06:53.490 --> 06:57.660
just so you know before git switch, it looked like this.

06:57.660 --> 07:00.390
It was slightly harder to remember and definitely clunkier.

07:00.390 --> 07:02.970
So git switch simplifies this a lot.

07:02.970 --> 07:04.500
If you wanna work on a branch

07:04.500 --> 07:08.129
that is on a GitHub repo, you don't have it locally,

07:08.129 --> 07:12.060
you just use git switch and then the name of the branch.

07:12.060 --> 07:13.350
So super simple.

07:13.350 --> 07:15.060
Let's try one more example.

07:15.060 --> 07:16.980
So I can view all those remote branches.

07:16.980 --> 07:19.230
git remote, no, what am I doing?

07:19.230 --> 07:22.961
git branch -r, let's go to the fantasy branch.

07:22.961 --> 07:24.720
I could check it out right

07:24.720 --> 07:27.146
and just go into detached head and take a look.

07:27.146 --> 07:30.300
Or I can make my own branch here

07:30.300 --> 07:32.250
that is tracking origin/fantasy

07:32.250 --> 07:35.370
and that's what I'm gonna do, git switch fantasy.

07:35.370 --> 07:36.810
Branch fantasy set up to track

07:36.810 --> 07:38.490
remote branch fantasy from origin.

07:38.490 --> 07:40.590
I'm on this branch fantasy now,

07:40.590 --> 07:42.570
I have three branches locally

07:42.570 --> 07:46.113
and over here, we see centaur and dragon.

07:46.980 --> 07:48.720
So let's make a change.

07:48.720 --> 07:51.450
I'm gonna add a new file in here.

07:51.450 --> 07:56.257
How about on this fantasy branch touch phoenix.txt?

07:57.960 --> 08:01.170
And I have a phoenix that I've copied

08:01.170 --> 08:03.300
from a free ASCII art website.

08:03.300 --> 08:05.880
Okay, so I'll add that.

08:05.880 --> 08:10.753
git add phoenix, git commit, add phoenix.txt.

08:13.440 --> 08:17.160
Alright, so what will I see when I type git status?

08:17.160 --> 08:19.320
Well, my fantasy branch is now ahead

08:19.320 --> 08:22.110
of origin/fantasy by one commit.

08:22.110 --> 08:23.610
Just like in the previous video,

08:23.610 --> 08:26.460
we had our main branch ahead of origin/main.

08:26.460 --> 08:28.230
So that means I have work on this machine.

08:28.230 --> 08:32.160
I have one commit on the fantasy branch that I can push up.

08:32.160 --> 08:35.520
So just like any other branch, git push

08:35.520 --> 08:37.590
and then the remote origin,

08:37.590 --> 08:39.060
and then the branch we wanna push

08:39.060 --> 08:41.520
and if I wanna push my new phoenix commit,

08:41.520 --> 08:43.290
it's on the fantasy branch.

08:43.290 --> 08:44.990
So that's the branch I'll push up.

08:46.110 --> 08:47.700
And then if I head back to GitHub

08:47.700 --> 08:50.310
and I switch to our fantasy branch,

08:50.310 --> 08:53.250
we now have phoenix showing up.

08:53.250 --> 08:55.380
Alright, so what I wanted to show in this video

08:55.380 --> 08:58.560
is that if we use the git switch command

08:58.560 --> 09:02.460
and then a name of a branch that may not exist locally yet

09:02.460 --> 09:07.020
but is a remote branch, if we use the correct matching name,

09:07.020 --> 09:08.790
Git will make us that branch locally

09:08.790 --> 09:12.060
and set it up automatically to track the remote branch

09:12.060 --> 09:13.230
with the same name.

09:13.230 --> 09:15.750
So that's really, really useful and very common.

09:15.750 --> 09:16.927
If somebody tells you,

09:16.927 --> 09:20.190
"Hey, I just pushed up a new branch called Bug Fix

09:20.190 --> 09:22.530
or Help me code, I'm stuck."

09:22.530 --> 09:24.540
Well I don't have that on my machine.

09:24.540 --> 09:28.380
I can easily get it by using git switch.

09:28.380 --> 09:30.360
Okay, so in the next video,

09:30.360 --> 09:33.333
we're now gonna talk about a new command called fetch.
